1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,508
Welcome to Code Quest, Chapter 1:

2
00:00:02,508 --> 00:00:05,318
Your First Program and
Your AI Study Partner.

3
00:00:05,318 --> 00:00:08,831
Today you will write and
run your first Python program,

4
00:00:08,831 --> 00:00:13,047
and you will learn to use
an AI assistant as a tutor.

5
00:00:13,817 --> 00:00:15,207
First, a puzzle.

6
00:00:15,207 --> 00:00:20,767
A learner asked an AI assistant
to print today's date as 2026,

7
00:00:20,767 --> 00:00:24,474
hyphen, 11, hyphen, 09,
using three separate values.

8
00:00:24,474 --> 00:00:27,717
The AI wrote this line of code.

9
00:00:28,483 --> 00:00:30,360
The code runs without an error.

10
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,862
But does it do what the learner asked?

11
00:00:37,617 --> 00:00:39,256
Here is the real output.

12
00:00:39,256 --> 00:00:41,880
There are spaces where
the hyphens should be.

13
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:45,160
Code that runs is not
always code that is correct.

14
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:48,439
By the end of this
chapter, you will know why,

15
00:00:48,439 --> 00:00:50,079
and how to fix it.

16
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:52,910
By the end of this chapter,

17
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:55,927
you will be able to run a Python file from

18
00:00:55,927 --> 00:00:57,737
the terminal and see its output.

19
00:00:57,737 --> 00:01:01,357
You will also be able to
explain how Python runs a program:

20
00:01:01,357 --> 00:01:04,072
one line at a time, from top to bottom.

21
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:07,618
You will use print
to show text and numbers,

22
00:01:07,618 --> 00:01:11,001
and control the spacing with
two settings called sep and end.

23
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:14,076
You will read an error
message and find the line

24
00:01:14,076 --> 00:01:14,999
that caused it.

25
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:18,529
And you will ask an
AI assistant to explain code

26
00:01:18,529 --> 00:01:20,197
in a safe and useful way.

27
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,674
Every app you use is a program.

28
00:01:23,674 --> 00:01:27,916
A program is a list of
instructions that a computer follows exactly.

29
00:01:27,916 --> 00:01:28,977
Your phone's calculator,

30
00:01:28,977 --> 00:01:32,511
a shop's checkout screen and
a train timetable all start

31
00:01:32,511 --> 00:01:35,693
as instructions like the
ones you will write today.

32
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:38,797
Output is the first skill you need.

33
00:01:38,797 --> 00:01:40,809
When a program shows a message,

34
00:01:40,809 --> 00:01:43,156
you can see what it is doing.

35
00:01:43,156 --> 00:01:46,844
Professional developers often use print
to check that their code works.

36
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:51,683
AI assistants such as ChatGPT
and Claude can now write code.

37
00:01:51,683 --> 00:01:54,270
You still need to understand that code.

38
00:01:54,270 --> 00:01:56,118
AI tools are often right,

39
00:01:56,118 --> 00:01:59,445
but they can also
be wrong while sounding confident.

40
00:01:59,445 --> 00:02:03,881
A study of AI tutors in
Harvard's CS50 course found exactly this.

41
00:02:03,881 --> 00:02:07,577
You are the person who
runs the code and decides

42
00:02:07,577 --> 00:02:09,055
if it is correct.

43
00:02:10,067 --> 00:02:13,717
A Python program is a
text file that ends in

44
00:02:13,717 --> 00:02:14,812
dot p y.

45
00:02:14,812 --> 00:02:16,637
Each line is an instruction.

46
00:02:16,637 --> 00:02:18,827
Python, the program
called the interpreter,

47
00:02:18,827 --> 00:02:22,477
reads your file and carries
out each instruction in order:

48
00:02:22,477 --> 00:02:25,397
top to bottom, one line at a time.

49
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:30,415
Print is a function: a
named action that Python already knows.

50
00:02:30,415 --> 00:02:33,905
You call it by
writing its name, then brackets.

51
00:02:33,905 --> 00:02:37,395
The value inside the
brackets is what it shows.

52
00:02:37,395 --> 00:02:39,722
Text must be inside quotation marks,

53
00:02:39,722 --> 00:02:43,212
and text in quotation
marks is called a string.

54
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:46,399
Think of a program like a recipe.

55
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:49,850
The cook follows each step
in order and does exactly

56
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:50,886
what it says.

57
00:02:50,886 --> 00:02:52,956
The comparison stops at one point.

58
00:02:52,956 --> 00:02:56,408
A cook can guess what
you meant, but Python cannot.

59
00:02:56,408 --> 00:02:58,823
If a step has a typing mistake,

60
00:02:58,823 --> 00:03:00,894
Python stops and reports an error.

61
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,550
Step 1: install Python.

62
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:08,086
Check the official guide for
your system on python dot org.

63
00:03:08,086 --> 00:03:10,561
The current version is Python 3.15,

64
00:03:10,561 --> 00:03:14,273
and any version from
3.12 works for this course.

65
00:03:14,273 --> 00:03:15,098
On Windows,

66
00:03:15,098 --> 00:03:19,222
install the Python install manager
from python dot org or

67
00:03:19,222 --> 00:03:20,459
the Microsoft Store.

68
00:03:21,217 --> 00:03:21,992
On macOS,

69
00:03:21,992 --> 00:03:26,255
download the installer package from
python dot org and open it.

70
00:03:26,255 --> 00:03:28,967
On Linux, Python
is usually installed already.

71
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:32,068
Then open a terminal
and check the version.

72
00:03:32,068 --> 00:03:35,943
You should see a line
that starts with Python 3.

73
00:03:36,967 --> 00:03:39,287
Step 2: create your first file.

74
00:03:39,287 --> 00:03:43,540
Open a code editor, such
as Visual Studio Code, or IDLE.

75
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:48,180
Create a file called ex 0
1 hello, and type these lines.

76
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:52,819
Line 1 starts with a hash
sign, so it is a comment.

77
00:03:52,819 --> 00:03:53,979
Python ignores comments.

78
00:03:53,979 --> 00:03:55,913
They are notes for people.

79
00:03:56,683 --> 00:03:58,728
Step 3: run the file.

80
00:03:58,728 --> 00:04:03,226
In the terminal, go to
the folder that holds your file.

81
00:04:03,226 --> 00:04:06,498
Type python, then the
file name, on Windows.

82
00:04:06,498 --> 00:04:10,587
Type python3, then the file
name, on macOS and Linux.

83
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:13,150
You will see two lines.

84
00:04:13,150 --> 00:04:15,670
Each print produces one line of output.

85
00:04:15,670 --> 00:04:17,831
The quotation marks do not appear,

86
00:04:17,831 --> 00:04:21,431
because they only mark where
the text starts and ends.

87
00:04:22,433 --> 00:04:24,449
Step 4: check the order.

88
00:04:24,449 --> 00:04:27,674
This program prints three
steps for making tea.

89
00:04:27,674 --> 00:04:30,496
Predict the output before you run it.

90
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:33,351
In which order will the lines appear?

91
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:41,644
The lines appear in the
same order as the code.

92
00:04:41,644 --> 00:04:45,525
Now swap two lines in
your file and run it again.

93
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:49,405
The output changes, because Python
always runs from top to bottom.

94
00:04:50,433 --> 00:04:53,152
Step 5: print several values at once.

95
00:04:53,152 --> 00:04:57,036
Print can take more than
one value, separated by commas.

96
00:04:57,036 --> 00:04:58,978
It also has two settings.

97
00:04:58,978 --> 00:05:01,697
Sep sets what goes between the values,

98
00:05:01,697 --> 00:05:04,028
and its default is one space.

99
00:05:04,028 --> 00:05:07,135
End sets what goes after the last value,

100
00:05:07,135 --> 00:05:09,854
and its default is a new line.

101
00:05:10,617 --> 00:05:12,102
Look at this program.

102
00:05:12,102 --> 00:05:15,443
Line 2 adds 3
and 4 without quotation marks.

103
00:05:15,443 --> 00:05:18,042
Line 3 sets sep to a hyphen.

104
00:05:18,042 --> 00:05:20,641
Line 4 sets end to three dots.

105
00:05:21,417 --> 00:05:24,900
Predict the output, especially
lines 4 and 5.

106
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:34,305
Line 2 has no quotation
marks around 3 plus 4,

107
00:05:34,305 --> 00:05:36,863
so Python calculates it and prints 7.

108
00:05:36,863 --> 00:05:41,249
Line 3 joins the values with
a hyphen instead of a space.

109
00:05:41,249 --> 00:05:45,269
Line 4 ends with three
dots instead of a new line,

110
00:05:45,269 --> 00:05:49,655
so line 5 continues on the
same line: Loading, dot dot dot,

111
00:05:49,655 --> 00:05:50,020
done.

112
00:05:51,033 --> 00:05:55,218
Here is a complete program
that prints a receipt for

113
00:05:55,218 --> 00:05:56,474
the Corner Cafe.

114
00:05:56,474 --> 00:05:59,822
It uses everything from
this chapter: print, text,

115
00:05:59,822 --> 00:06:02,751
sep for the
dotted lines, and calculations.

116
00:06:03,517 --> 00:06:06,053
Run it, and you get the receipt.

117
00:06:06,053 --> 00:06:10,400
The dots line up because each
sep value has a different length.

118
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:14,747
Python calculates the item count, 2,
and the total, 10, for you.

119
00:06:15,517 --> 00:06:18,603
Notice one problem: each
price is typed twice,

120
00:06:18,603 --> 00:06:22,074
once in its line and once in the total.

121
00:06:22,074 --> 00:06:26,317
In Chapter 2, you will
store prices in named values instead.

122
00:06:27,333 --> 00:06:30,925
Use an AI assistant
as a tutor that explains,

123
00:06:30,925 --> 00:06:34,118
not as a machine that does your work.

124
00:06:34,118 --> 00:06:36,512
Follow three safety habits from today.

125
00:06:36,512 --> 00:06:41,301
One: never paste passwords, keys or
private information into an AI tool.

126
00:06:41,301 --> 00:06:44,493
Two: run every piece of AI code yourself,

127
00:06:44,493 --> 00:06:48,085
and compare the output
with what you asked for.

128
00:06:48,085 --> 00:06:51,677
Three: you are responsible
for the code you use,

129
00:06:51,677 --> 00:06:54,071
even if an AI wrote it.

130
00:06:54,850 --> 00:06:56,977
AI helps at every stage.

131
00:06:56,977 --> 00:06:59,528
Plan the steps in plain English.

132
00:06:59,528 --> 00:07:03,782
Write the program yourself first,
then ask for an explanation.

133
00:07:03,782 --> 00:07:06,333
Generate code from an exact output.

134
00:07:06,333 --> 00:07:08,885
Debug with the full error message.

135
00:07:08,885 --> 00:07:10,587
Test by comparing outputs.

136
00:07:10,587 --> 00:07:13,564
And improve with at most two suggestions.

137
00:07:14,317 --> 00:07:16,023
Here is the Write card.

138
00:07:16,023 --> 00:07:19,778
Paste your code, and ask
the AI not to rewrite it.

139
00:07:19,778 --> 00:07:23,191
Ask it to explain each
line in one simple sentence,

140
00:07:23,191 --> 00:07:26,946
and to say whether it
prints exactly the output you want.

141
00:07:26,946 --> 00:07:28,311
Then run your program.

142
00:07:28,311 --> 00:07:31,383
If the AI said yes but the output differs,

143
00:07:31,383 --> 00:07:32,748
trust the real output.

144
00:07:33,517 --> 00:07:35,808
And here is the Generate card.

145
00:07:35,808 --> 00:07:38,100
Paste the exact output you want,

146
00:07:38,100 --> 00:07:41,155
and ask for code that uses only print.

147
00:07:41,155 --> 00:07:43,446
Then compare every
character, including spaces.

148
00:07:43,446 --> 00:07:46,502
This is how the date flaw was caught.

149
00:07:47,283 --> 00:07:49,200
Now the AI pair moment.

150
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,117
Back to the opening puzzle.

151
00:07:51,117 --> 00:07:54,950
The request asked for hyphens,
but the output has spaces.

152
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:57,250
Which setting did the AI forget?

153
00:07:58,017 --> 00:07:59,921
Think about step 5.

154
00:08:06,683 --> 00:08:10,345
The AI forgot that sep
is a space by default.

155
00:08:10,345 --> 00:08:14,739
The fix sets sep to a
hyphen, and the date prints correctly.

156
00:08:14,739 --> 00:08:19,133
Always check AI output against what
you asked for, character by character.

157
00:08:20,133 --> 00:08:21,614
Now, four common mistakes.

158
00:08:21,614 --> 00:08:24,577
Mistake 1 is a
missing closing quotation mark.

159
00:08:24,577 --> 00:08:27,169
Python cannot see where the text ends,

160
00:08:27,169 --> 00:08:29,390
so it reports a Syntax Error.

161
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:33,093
A syntax error means Python
could not read the file,

162
00:08:33,093 --> 00:08:34,574
so no line runs.

163
00:08:34,574 --> 00:08:39,018
The fix is to add the
closing quotation mark before the bracket.

164
00:08:39,783 --> 00:08:42,801
Mistake 2 is a capital letter in print.

165
00:08:42,801 --> 00:08:47,328
Python treats capital P Print and
small p print as different names.

166
00:08:47,328 --> 00:08:49,591
Notice that Start was printed first.

167
00:08:49,591 --> 00:08:52,609
This error happened while
the program was running,

168
00:08:52,609 --> 00:08:55,627
so the lines before it had already run.

169
00:08:55,627 --> 00:08:59,399
The message names line 3,
and even suggests the fix.

170
00:09:00,150 --> 00:09:02,559
Mistake 3 is curly quotation marks.

171
00:09:02,559 --> 00:09:06,575
Word processors and some chat
apps change straight quotation marks

172
00:09:06,575 --> 00:09:07,780
into curly ones.

173
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:09,787
Python only accepts straight ones.

174
00:09:09,787 --> 00:09:13,401
Type the quotation marks
again in your code editor,

175
00:09:13,401 --> 00:09:18,220
and copy code from a code
block, never from a formatted document.

176
00:09:18,983 --> 00:09:21,840
Mistake 4 is a missing closing bracket.

177
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:24,697
Every opening bracket
needs a closing bracket.

178
00:09:25,450 --> 00:09:29,228
The habit to build: read the
last line of an error first.

179
00:09:29,228 --> 00:09:31,117
It names the kind of error.

180
00:09:31,117 --> 00:09:34,580
Then look at the line
number to find where it happened.

181
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:38,170
Two questions to
think beyond this chapter.

182
00:09:38,170 --> 00:09:38,538
First:

183
00:09:38,538 --> 00:09:42,209
you want to print a
shopping list with each item

184
00:09:42,209 --> 00:09:45,147
on its own line, using only one print.

185
00:09:45,147 --> 00:09:47,717
Which setting from
this chapter could help?

186
00:09:47,717 --> 00:09:48,819
Test your idea.

187
00:09:55,583 --> 00:09:59,283
Second: an AI gives you
code that runs with no error.

188
00:09:59,283 --> 00:10:02,646
What two things should you
check before you trust it?

189
00:10:09,667 --> 00:10:10,520
Let's recap.

190
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:13,506
A program is a list of instructions,

191
00:10:13,506 --> 00:10:18,626
and Python runs them one at
a time, from top to bottom.

192
00:10:18,626 --> 00:10:19,906
Print shows values.

193
00:10:19,906 --> 00:10:22,039
Text goes inside quotation marks,

194
00:10:22,039 --> 00:10:25,879
and calculations without quotation
marks are worked out first.

195
00:10:26,650 --> 00:10:28,843
Sep controls what goes between values,

196
00:10:28,843 --> 00:10:31,401
and end controls what goes after them.

197
00:10:31,401 --> 00:10:35,421
An error message names the
kind of error and the line,

198
00:10:35,421 --> 00:10:37,613
so read the last line first.

199
00:10:37,613 --> 00:10:40,902
And AI is a tutor: ask it to explain,

200
00:10:40,902 --> 00:10:43,095
then run and check everything yourself.

201
00:10:43,850 --> 00:10:46,776
You earned 100 XP and the First Run badge.

202
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:48,698
Now practise.

203
00:10:48,698 --> 00:10:51,392
The practice file has eight tasks.

204
00:10:51,392 --> 00:10:55,881
The warm-up tasks are worth
10 XP each: introduce yourself,

205
00:10:55,881 --> 00:10:59,473
fix a broken greeting,
and predict, then run.

206
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:03,211
The apply tasks are worth 20 XP each,

207
00:11:03,211 --> 00:11:06,542
and the two stretch
tasks are worth 40 each.

208
00:11:06,542 --> 00:11:08,022
In the last task,

209
00:11:08,022 --> 00:11:11,724
you check an AI's work
and fix every difference yourself.

210
00:11:11,724 --> 00:11:14,685
Try each task before
you open the solution.

211
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:19,562
In Chapter 2, you will
give your data names with variables,

212
00:11:19,562 --> 00:11:23,073
so a program can store
a value once and use

213
00:11:23,073 --> 00:11:24,127
it many times.

214
00:11:24,127 --> 00:11:25,882
Do the warm-up tasks now.

215
00:11:25,882 --> 00:11:27,638
They take about ten minutes.

216
00:11:27,638 --> 00:11:29,393
See you in Chapter 2.
